/* libjbn profile: x86-64 with BMI2 and ADX (Intel Broadwell and later, AMD
   Zen and later; not under Rosetta). Required once, at the top level of a
   program, before the instances (README, "Profiles and the selection of
   the multiplication").

   JBN_FEATURES:   BMI2 and ADX, so the multiplication rows use
                   MULX/ADCX/ADOX.
   JBN_UNROLL_MAX: the multiplication, squaring and Montgomery reduction of
                   a size scope (bn_mulsel@n.jinc) are unrolled when
                   nlimbs <= JBN_UNROLL_MAX, and loop over their rows above.
                   16: on an Intel Core i7-14700K the unrolled bn_mulx is 8%
                   to 11% faster than the looped bn_mulx_loop on an
                   exponentiation up to 16 limbs, and 59% slower at 32 limbs,
                   where its code no longer fits the 32 KB instruction cache.
                   Sizes between 16 and 32 limbs were not timed; the
                   crossover is expected near 24 limbs on that CPU, and
                   depends on the size of the instruction cache.
   A size scope may shadow JBN_UNROLL_MAX (`param int JBN_UNROLL_MAX = 0;`
   inside it, for instance, for the smallest code at that size).
*/

from Libjbn require "jbn_features.jinc"

param int JBN_FEATURES = JBN_FEATURES_MULX;
param int JBN_UNROLL_MAX = 16;
